 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-10-24 (7): every biome has its own water colour: rivers, lakes and the sea take the tint of the land they lie in (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen on a device; +3 tests: `biome::tests::every_biome_has_a_water_colour`, `mesh::tests::water_carries_the_biome_colour`, `chunks::tests::water_colours_blend_across_a_border`). NOT b1.7.3 (one water colour for all there); a rendering addition on top of the river work.
+    - Colours (`Biome::water_color`, all 19 biomes): the game's own per-biome `water_color` values (1.13 to 1.17 set: jungle `14A2C5`, swamp `617B64`, forest `1E97F2`, plains `44AFF5`, taiga `287097`, desert `32A598`, savanna `2C8B9C`, tundra `3D57D6`, ocean `1787D4`, beach `157CAB`, mountains `007BF7`, cherry grove `5DB7EF`, default `3F76E4`); the biomes this world folds together and the six Blooming ones take their nearest cousin.
+    - Blend (`chunks::biome_tints` -> `Tints::water`, `blend_water`): a column's colour is the mean over the 7 x 7 columns round it (`WATER_BLEND` 3), read from an area padded by 3, so a river crossing a biome border shifts tint over a few blocks and two chunks agree at their seam.
+    - Mesh: `tint_of` returns the column's water colour for ids 8 / 9, so every water vertex carries it (the 12-byte vertex keeps it as R5G6B5). Far water: `lod::column_into` keeps it on the water span (in steps of 8, the tile's palette holds 255 tints), the sheet quad takes it from the voxel's palette entry; LoD tile magic `L8` -> `L9`.
+    - Shaders (`fs_water` in `gpu/pipeline.rs`, its twin in `gles/shaders.rs`; `water_rgb`): the water tile is RECOLOURED, not multiplied (it is a saturated blue, 46 67 244, a multiply would only darken it): the tile's luminance over its average (0.1085 in linear light) times the biome colour, so the waves keep their light and dark and an average texel is exactly the biome's colour. White (no biome) leaves the tile alone; the LoD debug sheet (tile 1066) still multiplies its cyan.
+    - Not done: Photon's `render/water.rs` (not wired into `lib.rs`) does not read it; no per-river tint (a river takes its biome's, there is no `StreamTint`); no ice / frozen-river variant; the swamp's water is not murkier. UNVERIFIED: the compile, the colour numbers (from memory of the game's biome files), whether `atlas_sample_g` returns linear light (the 0.1085 average assumes it), how a border looks on a device. Chunks already generated need nothing (the tint is read at mesh time); far tiles are remade through the magic.
 - 2026-10-24 (6): shoulder-surf: glides in from the vanilla third-person view, and the player no longer drifts onto the crosshair when strafing (written WITHOUT a toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen on a device; +2 tests in `render::shoulder`: `cargo test --lib shoulder` first; `python3 tools/shoulder_proto.py` runs both fixes in Python and passes).
     - Order of views: first person -> vanilla third (4 blocks behind) -> shoulder surf, so the dolly out from the head is gone. The blend (`Rig::blend`, omega 16, about 0.3 s) now runs from the vanilla camera place to the shoulder shot: `desired = pivot - fwd * (THIRD + (dist - THIRD) * w)`, `THIRD = 4 + RADIUS` (the pull-in stops `RADIUS` short of the wanted place even in open air, so `4 + RADIUS` lands on vanilla's 4.0 to the block). The first frame also takes the pull-in fraction (`frac`) from the ray at once (`prime`), so a wall behind the player does not start the glide with the camera inside it. The field-of-view whip on the way in is removed (a 10 % whip over a 1.4 block slide was out of scale); the sprint / speed widening stays. `Shot::close` is no longer set at the start (the model is drawn from the first frame, like the vanilla view); it still is in a tight space.
     - Strafing: the pivot's xz lag (up to the 0.6 cap) is along the strafe, so strafing toward the camera's shoulder (right shoulder, strafe right) took the whole 0.62 offset away and put the player on the crosshair. The lateral part of the lag that points toward the head's line is now dropped (the pivot's world x / z shifted along `right`); lag to the far side stays, and so does all the forward / back lag and the 0.6 cap. Not applied while the shoulders are swapping (`|side_s - side| < 0.05`), since the offset crosses the head then. Standing is unchanged: the player never gets nearer the crosshair than there. Python check: camera offset while strafing 0.02 before, 0.62 after.
